{
  "id": "mg-transformers",
  "title": "Transformers",
  "description": "Ideal transformers, impedance reflection and the regulation and efficiency of real ones.",
  "reference": "Hambley §15.5–15.6",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "The ideal transformer",
      "body": "Two coils on a common iron core. 'Ideal' means: no winding resistance, all flux links both coils, and a core of zero reluctance (no magnetizing current, no core loss). The same $d\\phi/dt$ then induces voltages in proportion to the turns. With $i_1$ into the primary dot and $i_2$ **out of** the secondary dot, the net mmf $N_1i_1 - N_2i_2$ must be zero, which gives the current ratio.",
      "formula": "\\frac{v_2}{v_1} = \\frac{N_2}{N_1}, \\qquad \\frac{i_2}{i_1} = \\frac{N_1}{N_2}",
      "circuit": {
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "acsource",
            "from": [
              0,
              2
            ],
            "to": [
              0,
              0
            ],
            "label": "V_s"
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                0,
                0
              ],
              [
                3,
                0
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                3,
                2
              ],
              [
                0,
                2
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "transformer",
            "at": [
              4,
              1
            ],
            "label": "N_1 : N_2",
            "flip": false
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                5,
                0
              ],
              [
                7,
                0
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "resistor",
            "from": [
              7,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              7,
              2
            ],
            "label": "R_L"
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                7,
                2
              ],
              [
                5,
                2
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "arrow",
            "from": [
              2.1,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              2.9,
              0
            ],
            "label": "i_1"
          },
          {
            "type": "arrow",
            "from": [
              5.4,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              6.4,
              0
            ],
            "label": "i_2"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              2.5,
              0.45
            ],
            "label": "+"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              2.5,
              1
            ],
            "label": "v_1"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              2.5,
              1.55
            ],
            "label": "−"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              5.6,
              0.45
            ],
            "label": "+"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              5.6,
              1
            ],
            "label": "v_2"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "at": [
              5.6,
              1.55
            ],
            "label": "−"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Power is conserved",
      "body": "$p_2 = v_2i_2 = \\frac{N_2}{N_1}v_1\\cdot\\frac{N_1}{N_2}i_1 = p_1$: an ideal transformer neither creates nor consumes power. Stepping the voltage **up** steps the current **down** by the same factor. That is why power is transmitted at hundreds of kV: for the same power, line current falls by the step-up ratio and the $I^2R$ line loss by its square.",
      "formula": "V_1I_1 = V_2I_2"
    },
    {
      "title": "Reflecting impedances",
      "body": "Seen from the primary, a load $Z_L$ on the secondary looks like $(N_1/N_2)^2Z_L$. A step-down transformer therefore makes a small load look large. The reverse also works: a source $V_s$ with internal impedance $Z_s$ on the primary looks, from the secondary, like a source $V_s\\,N_2/N_1$ with impedance $Z_s(N_2/N_1)^2$. For maximum power transfer to a resistive load choose $(N_1/N_2)^2R_L = R_s$.",
      "formula": "Z_L' = \\left(\\frac{N_1}{N_2}\\right)^2Z_L"
    },
    {
      "title": "Real transformer model",
      "body": "A real transformer is an ideal one plus:\n- $R_1$, $R_2$: winding (copper) resistances.\n- $X_1$, $X_2$: leakage reactances (flux that links only one winding).\n- $X_m$: magnetizing reactance (current needed to set up the core flux).\n- $R_c$: core-loss resistance (hysteresis and eddy currents).",
      "circuit": {
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              0,
              0
            ],
            "label": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "terminal",
            "at": [
              0,
              2
            ],
            "label": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "resistor",
            "from": [
              0,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              1,
              0
            ],
            "label": "R_1"
          },
          {
            "type": "inductor",
            "from": [
              1,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              2,
              0
            ],
            "label": "X_1"
          },
          {
            "type": "inductor",
            "from": [
              2,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              2,
              2
            ],
            "label": "X_m",
            "flip": true
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                2,
                0
              ],
              [
                4,
                0
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "resistor",
            "from": [
              3,
              0
            ],
            "to": [
              3,
              2
            ],
            "label": "R_c"
          },
          {
            "type": "wire",
            "points": [
              [
                0,
                2
              ],
              [
                4,
                2
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "node",
            "at": [
              2,
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "node",
            "at": [
              3,
              0
            ]
